  "body": "## What changed\n\n- The cutting table has six new work materials: `softwood`, `hardwood`, `mdf`, `plywood`, `acrylic` and `hdpe`.\n- Each new row has a cutting-speed window and a 10 mm chip load for HSS and carbide. Each row also has Kienzle constants.\n- The new rows are appended. The table is indexed in `WorkMaterial::ALL` order. A project file stores the word, so old files read the same.\n- New module `crates/tools/src/cutting_router.rs` gives the router chip load by cutter diameter. The points are 3 mm 0.30, 6 mm 0.65, 10 mm 1.0 and 12 mm 1.15 of the 10 mm chip load. Below 3 mm the scale falls in proportion to the diameter. Above 12 mm it holds.\n- `WorkMaterial::chip_scale(d)` selects the router curve for a router material. It selects the existing metal bands (`fz_scale`) for the other materials.\n- `app::cutting::compute` uses `chip_scale`. On a router material, a V-bit (`ToolKind::Taper`) reads its chip load at half its diameter.\n- The turning table has the same six rows, because it is also indexed in `ALL` order.\n- The `camcraft-tools speeds` command, the MCP tool descriptions, the generated `docs/agents/mcp-tools.md` and the `camcraft-mcp` skill list the new words.\n\nThe rpm clamp is the existing code in `compute`. It clamps to `spindle_min` and `spindle_max`. It then calculates the feed again at the clamped rpm, so the chip load stays the same. It names each clamp in `notes`. This PR does not change that code. The new tests pin it for a router spindle.\n\n## Cause\n\nThe table had only aluminium, steels, cast iron, brass and POM. A router user who cut wood or plastics got no suggestion, or a suggestion from a metal row. The metal bands also give each cutter from 3 to 6 mm the same chip load. For wood, that is approximately two times too much for a 1/8 in cutter.\n\n## Tests\n\nNew suite `crates/app/tests/cutting_router.rs`:\n\n- `router_materials_have_words_and_round_trip`\n- `each_router_material_matches_the_hand_calculation_on_a_6mm_end_mill`: S and F for each material against a hand calculation.\n- `end_mill_v_bit_and_ball_nose_stay_inside_a_router_spindle`: end mills of 3, 6 and 12 mm, a V-bit and a ball nose, for each material and tool material, on a 10000-24000 rpm spindle.\n- `a_v_bit_reads_its_chip_load_at_half_its_diameter`\n- `a_router_spindle_clamps_both_ways_and_keeps_the_chip_load`: 3 mm softwood is held at 24000 rpm, with F 4320. 12 mm HSS acrylic is raised to 10000 rpm, with F 2875. Each clamp is named.\n\nUnit tests in `crates/tools/src/cutting_router.rs` pin the curve points, the ends and that the curve never falls.\n\nBefore the fix, the suite did not compile: the materials did not exist.\n\nMutations (each put back, each run, then restored):\n\n- Old `crates/app/src/cutting.rs` (metal bands for all materials): 3 tests red.\n- No half-diameter V-bit reading: `a_v_bit_reads_its_chip_load_at_half_its_diameter` red.\n- `chip_scale` always returns `fz_scale`: 3 tests red.\n\nGate: `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` is green on the final head (24 steps). The first run had one expected red, `mcp_doc_drift`, because the MCP description changed. I generated `docs/agents/mcp-tools.md` again, and the suite is green. `cargo clippy -p camcraft-tools -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean. `cargo test -p camcraft-ui --lib -- material cutting` is green (7 tests), because the UI iterates `WorkMaterial::ALL`.\n\nHeavy lanes: `cargo xtask gate heavy --base upstream/main` ran on the final head `a022f26da77172be7524d3bf7dab8ac8de98ed84`. Result: 236 steps, 0 failed, wall 44.3 min.\n\n- Step 1: the release build of `camcraft-motion` and `camcraft-app` (`--no-run`).\n- `motion` heavy lanes: 37 steps, all green.\n- `app` heavy lanes: 198 steps, all green.\n- Known-fail markers stayed red as expected: #1950 (3 tests) and #2849 (2 tests).\n\n## Goldens moved\n\nNone. The metal rows use the same expression as before, so their S and F do not change.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- I did not start the GUI. The material lists in the UI come from `WorkMaterial::ALL`, and their unit tests are green.\n- The turning rows for wood and plastics have only the table-order test. No lathe test uses them.\n- The numbers are our own conservative values at the low side of public woodworking and plastics ranges. A human must still check them against a real router cut.\n\n## Overlaps\n\n- #3151 changes `crates/app/src/cutting.rs` in `feed_mut`. This PR changes `compute` in the same file. The functions are different. The PR that merges second refreshes.\n\nDecisions: `docs/decisions/3068-router-materials.md`.\n\nProcess change: yes\n\nEffort: high\n\nChangelog: Cutting data for softwood, hardwood, MDF, plywood, acrylic and HDPE, with the chip load by cutter diameter and the router spindle clamp.\n\nFixes #3068\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
